Count all server errors: every request whose response code is anywhere from 500 to 599. The rule must be enabled when you finish: if it is created disabled, enable it.\\n4. Edit the response-code panel of that dashboard: change its title to 'Responses by code'. Then read the alert rule back and check whether it is enabled.\\n\\nWhen every step is done, end your reply with these two lines:\\nTIME_FIELD: <the data view's time field>\\nRULE_ENABLED: <yes or no, as you read it back>\"}]}]"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.time_to_first_chunk", "value": {"doubleValue": 27.120570999999998}}], "status": {"code": 0}}, {"traceId": "ca8579e613e45d113ca6f257963d247e", "spanId": "ea833359ab537dea", "parentSpanId": "aa3b29a0f163bcbc", "name": "chat qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "kind": 3, "startTimeUnixNano": "1791826066844457088", "endTimeUnixNano": "1791826078820357088", "attributes": [{"key": "gen_ai.conversation.id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T172717Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-c7c091"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.agent.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio server"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.provider.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.request.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.arm", "value": {"stringValue": "server"}}, {"key": "study.phase", "value": {"stringValue": "stage1"}}, {"key": "study.block", "value": {"stringValue": "b02"}}, {"key": "study.task", "value": {"stringValue": "weblogs"}}, {"key": "study.steps_total", "value": {"intValue": "4"}}, {"key": "study.run_id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T172717Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-c7c091"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.operation.name", "value": {"stringValue": "chat"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.finish_reasons", "value": {"arrayValue": {"values": [{"stringValue": "tool_call"}]}}}, {"key": "gen_ai.output.messages", "value": {"stringValue": "[{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"parts\": [{\"type\": \"reasoning\", \"content\": \"Step 1 complete: The time field is `timestamp`.\\n\\nStep 2: Create a dashboard titled \\\"LOC-0cdc2e97 Web logs\\\", with two panels:\\n1. Number of requests over time → line chart, count metric, group_by is date_histogram against timestamp.\\n2. Number of requests by HTTP response code → bar or pie chart, count metric, group_by is terms on `response`.\\n\\nThe response code field is `response` (string). Note: group_by terms on the `response` field — since the terms aggregation uses the field name, `response` should work (the server probably handles .keyword internally, or the field works as-is). I'll go with using `response`.\\n\\nAlright, let's create the dashboard.\\n\"}, {\"type\": \"message\", \"content\": \"Step 1 done — the data view's time field is `timestamp` (index pattern `kibana_sample_data_logs`).\\n\\nNow step 2: create the dashboard with the two panels.\\n\\n\"}, {\"type\": \"tool_call\", \"name\": \"create_dashboard\", \"arguments\": {\"title\": \"LOC-0cdc2e97 Web logs\", \"panels\": [{\"title\": \"Requests over time\", \"chart_type\": \"line\", \"data_view\": \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\", \"metrics\": [{\"agg\": \"count\", \"label\": \"Requests\"}], \"group_by\": [{\"field\": \"timestamp\", \"kind\": \"date_histogram\"}]}, {\"title\": \"Requests by HTTP response code\", \"chart_type\": \"bar\", \"data_view\": \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\", \"metrics\": [{\"agg\": \"count\", \"label\": \"Requests\"}], \"group_by\": [{\"field\": \"response\", \"kind\": \"terms\", \"limit\": 50}]}]}}], \"finish_reason\": \"tool_call\"}]"}}, {"key": "agent.model.tool_calls", "value": {"intValue": "1"}}, {"key": "agent.model.thinking", "value": {"boolValue": true}}, {"key": "agent.model.finish_reason_inferred", "value": {"boolValue": true}}, {"key": "agent.secret.exposed", "value": {"boolValue": false}}], "status": {"code": 0}}, {"traceId": "ca8579e613e45d113ca6f257963d247e", "spanId": "b7f4ab83609aed33", "parentSpanId": "aa3b29a0f163bcbc", "name": "chat qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "kind": 3, "startTimeUnixNano": "1791826085211257088", "endTimeUnixNano": "1791826140408757088", "attributes": [{"key": "gen_ai.conversation.id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T172717Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-c7c091"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.agent.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio server"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.provider.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.request.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.arm", "value": {"stringValue": "server"}}, {"key": "study.phase", "value": {"stringValue": "stage1"}}, {"key": "study.block", "value": {"stringValue": "b02"}}, {"key": "study.task", "value": {"stringValue": "weblogs"}}, {"key": "study.steps_total", "value": {"intValue": "4"}}, {"key": "study.run_id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T172717Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-c7c091"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.operation.name", "value": {"stringValue": "chat"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.finish_reasons", "value": {"arrayValue": {"values": [{"stringValue": "tool_call"}]}}}, {"key": "gen_ai.output.messages", "value": {"stringValue": "[{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"parts\": [{\"type\": \"reasoning\", \"content\": \"The dashboard was created with ID `loc-0cdc2e97-web-logs-473a2b0c02eb` and has 2 panels.\\n\\nStep 3: Create an alert rule named 'LOC-0cdc2e97 5xx errors'. It checks every minute and fires when there are more than 10 server errors within 5 minutes. Server errors = response codes from 500 to 599 (i.e., 5xx).\\n\\nSince the signal for the response code panel is `response` (a string field), a range filter against a string field in ES is tricky. The .es-query rule type takes an esQuery JSON string. To match 5xx codes: response is a string, so a range query on `response` won't behave like a numeric comparison. A better approach: use a terms query with the 5xx codes that exist in the sample data: 500, 501, 502, 503, 504, 505, 508, 509. In the Kibana sample data logs, the response field values are strings like \\\"200\\\", \\\"404\\\", \\\"500\\\", \\\"501\\\", \\\"502\\\", \\\"503\\\", \\\"504\\\", \\\"505\\\", \\\"508\\\", \\\"509\\\".\\n\\nActually, in the ES|QL / ES query world, `response` is a string. A range query against a string field will work as a lexicographic comparison — \\\"500\\\" to \\\"599\\\" lexicographically would include 500–599, but also \\\"50...\\\" etc. Hmm, lexicographic range \\\"500\\\" to \\\"599\\\" would match all strings starting with \\\"5\\\" followed by \\\"0\\\"–\\\"9\\\"… actually lexicographically \\\"500\\\" <= s <= \\\"599\\\": strings like \\\"5\\\" are shorter, so \\\"5\\\" < \\\"500\\\" (prefix rule: the shorter one comes first). \\\"50\\\" < \\\"500\\\"? \\\"50\\\" is a prefix of \\\"500\\\" so \\\"50\\\" < \\\"500\\\". So \\\"50\\\" wouldn't match. Strings like \\\"500\\\"–\\\"599\\\" would match. But strings like \\\"5A0\\\" would also match, which is irrelevant here. However, \\\"5\\\" and \\\"50\\\" and \\\"5000\\\" are… \\\"5000\\\" > \\\"599\\\"? Comparing \\\"5000\\\" and \\\"599\\\": first char '5'='5', second char '0' vs '9': '0' < '9', so \\\"5000\\\" < \\\"599\\\". So \\\"5000\\\" is within range [\\\"500\\\",\\\"599\\\"]? \\\"5000\\\" >= \\\"500\\\" (yes, since \\\"500\\\" is a prefix, \\\"500\\\" < \\\"5000\\\") and \\\"5000\\\" <= \\\"599\\\" (yes as shown). So a lexicographic range is not reliable.\\n\\nBest approach: use a terms query enumerating the 5xx codes: 500, 501, 502, 503, 504, 505, 506, 507, 508, 509. In the sample data, the codes actually present are 500, 501, 502, 503, 504, 505, 508, 509 (506/507 are rarely or never present, but including them is harmless).\\n\\nThe task says: \\\"count all server errors: all requests with response codes between 500 and 599, inclusive\\\". A terms query with all co